  // the wind, it is RUNNING SHOREWARD, and −shoreN is that direction. The ramp
  // deliberately keeps the drift at full strength right through the waterline, so
  // the wash is CARRIED PAST it and destroyed by the sd < 0 clamp at the bottom of
  // this shader — the water soaking into the sand. r4 ramped the drift down to
  // zero at sd = 0.7 m instead, and the convergence built a permanent 5 m foam
  // band along the whole waterline that was the last thing in the frame still
  // reading as PAINT. (r5 tried the physically-tidier answer — signing this drift
  // with the set phase so it surged and drained — and it drained the zone
  // completely: adjacent sd bands pushed opposite ways, so the field never
  // accumulated anywhere. The advance/retreat read now lives in the WASH FRONT
  // below, which states its position explicitly instead of hoping a divergent
  // velocity field will produce one.)
  // …and the bore's speed is the state's, not a constant: a 30 cm coastal swash
  // slides in at walking pace and a storm bore runs. A constant 2.8 m/s smeared
  // coastal's whole 6 m of swash zone into one even sheet in 2 s.
  // Halved again at r14: at 1.74 m/s a coastal parcel laid an 8.7 m trail over its
  // 5 s life, which spread every swash's residue across the WHOLE surf zone and
